[Music]  near a great forest dwelt a poor  woodcutter of his wife and his two  children the boy was called Hansel and  the girl Gretel he had little to bite  ten to break and when the great scarcity  fell on the land he could no longer  procure daily bread now when he thought  over this at night in his bed and tossed  about in his anxiety he groaned and said  to his wife what is to become of us how  are we to feed our poor children when we  no longer have anything even for  ourselves I'll tell you what husband  answered the woman early tomorrow  morning we shall take the children into  the forest where it is the thickest  there we will light a fire for them and  give them each one piece of bread and  then we will go to work and leave them  alone they will not find their way home  again and we shall be done with them no  wife said the man how can I here to  leave my children alone in the forest  the wild animals would soon come and  tear them to pieces  oh you fool she said then we must all  four die of hunger you may as well plain  the planks to our coffins now and she  left him no peace until he consented but  I feel very sorry for the poor children  all the same said the man the two  children had also not been able to sleep  for hunger and had heard what their  stepmother had said to their father  Gretel wept bitter tears and said to  Hansel now all is over for us  hush Gretel said Hansel do not distress  yourself I will soon find a way to help  us and when the old folks had fallen  asleep he got up and put on his little  coat opened the door below and crept  outside and the moon shone brightly and  the white pebbles which lay in front of  the house glistened like real silver  pennies Hansel stooped and put as many  of them in his little pocket as he could  then he went back and said to Gretel be  comforted dear little sister and sleep  in peace God will not forsake us  and he lay down again in his bed when  the day dawned but before the Sun had  risen the woman came in a woke the two  children  get up you sluggards will go into the  forest to fetch some wood  she gave each of them a little piece of  bread there is something for your dinner  but do not eat it before 4:00 you'll get  nothing else  Gretel took the bread under her apron as  Hansel still had the stones in his  pocket then they all set out together on  the way to the forest when they had  walked a short time Hansel Stood Still  one peeped back at the house and did so  again and again his father said Hansel  what are you looking at there and  staying behind for mind yourself and do  not forget to use your legs father said  Hansel I'm looking at my little white  cat which is sitting on the roof and  wants to say goodbye to me the wife  turned fool that is not your little cat  that is the Morning Sun shining on the  chimneys Hansel however had not been  looking back at his cats but had been  constantly throwing one of the white  pebbles out of his pocket and onto the  road when they had reached the middle of  the forest to the farthest end now  children pile up some wood and I will  light a fire for you so you may not be  called Hansel and Gretel gathered the  brushwood together as high as a little  hill the brushwood was lit and when the  flames were burnin very high the woman  said now children lay yourselves down by  the fire and rest we will go into the  forest and cut some wood when we had  done we will come back and fetch you  away Hansel and Gretel sat by the fire  and when no one came each ate a little  piece of bread and as they heard the  strokes of the wood axe they believed  that their father was near it was not  however the axe it was a branch which  had been fastened to a withered tree  which the wind was blowing backwards and  forwards and as they had been sitting  for such a long time their eyes shot to  a fatigue and they fell fast asleep when  at last they awoke it was already dark  night Gretel began to cry  said how are we to get out of the forest  now but Hansel comforted his sister and  sent just wait a little until the moon  has risen and then we will soon find the  way and when the full moon had risen  Hansel took his little sister by the  hand and followed the pebbles which  shone like newly coined silver pieces  and showed them the way they walked the  whole night long and by break of day  they once more came to their father's  house they knocked at the door and when  the woman opened it and saw it was  Hansel and Gretel she said you naughty  children  why have you slept so long in the forest  we thought you were never coming back at  all the father however rejoiced for it  had to cut him to the heart to leave  them behind alone not long afterwards  there was another great scarcity and the  children heard their mother at night say  to their father everything is eaten  again we have one half loaf left and  then there is no more the children must  go we will take them farther into the  wood so that they will never find their  way out again there is no other means of  saving ourselves the man's heart was  heavy and he thought to himself it would  be better for you to share your last  mouthful of your children the woman  however would listen to nothing he had  to say but scolded and reproached him he  who says a must say be likewise and as  he had yielded for the first time he had  to do so for a second time also the  children however was still awake and had  heard the entire conversation when the  old folks had fallen asleep again Hansel  again got up and wanted to go outside to  pick pebbles as he did before but the  woman had locked the door and Hansel  could not get out nevertheless he  comforted his sister and said do not cry  Gretel go to sleep quietly and the good  God will help us early in the morning  came the woman and took the children out  of their bed there bit of bread was  given to them but it was so much smaller  than a time before on the way into the  forest Hansel crumbled he's in his  pocket and often stood and threw a  morsel onto the ground  Hansol why do you stop and look around  said the father go on I am looking back  at My Little Pigeon which is sitting on  the roof and wishes to say goodbye to me  answered Hansel simpleton said the woman  and that is not your Little Pigeon that  is the Morning Sun that is shining on  the chimney a Hansel however little by  little through all of the crumbs onto  the path the woman led the children  still deeper into the forest where they  had never in their lives been before  then a great fire was again made and the  mother said  just sit here you children and when you  are tired you may sleep a little we are  going into the forest to cut wood and in  the evening when we are done we will  return to fetch you away when it was  noon Gretel shared a piece of her bread  of Hansel who had scattered he's by the  way the Nathan asleep and the evening  came and went but no one came for the  pond children they did not wake until it  was dark night and Hansel comforted his  sister and said just wait Gretel until  the moon rises and then we shall see the  crumbs of bread which I had strewn about  they will show us the way home again  when the moon came and they set out they  found no crumbs for the many thousands  of birds which fly about in the woods in  the fields had eaten them all Hansel  said to Gretel we shall soon find the  way but they did not find it they walked  the whole night and the next day from  morning to the evening but they did not  get out of the forest and were very  hungry for they had nothing to eat but  two or three berries which had grown on  the ground and as they were so weary  that their legs would carry them no  longer they lay down beneath a tree and  fell asleep it was three mornings since  they had left their father's house they  began to walk again but they always got  deeper into the forest and if help did  not come soon they would die of hunger  and weariness when it was midday they  saw a beautiful snow-white bird sitting  on a bough which sang so delightfully  that they stood still and listened to it  and when it had finished its song it  spread its wings and flew away before  them they followed the bird until they  reached a little house when they came  closer to the little house they saw that  it was made of bread and covered in cake  and the windows were made of clear sugar  we were set to work on that said Hansel  and have a good meal I will eat a piece  of the roof I knew Gretel you can eat  some of the window it should taste sweet  Hansel reached above and broke off a  little piece of the roof to see how it  tasted and Gretel leaned against the  window and nibbled at the panes then a  soft voice cried from the room  nibble nibble gnaw who is nibbling at my  little house the children answered the  wind the wind the heaven-born wind and  they went on eating without disturbing  themselves Hansel who thought the roof  tasted very nice tore down a great piece  of it and Gretel pushed out the hole of  one round windowpane sat down and  enjoyed herself suddenly the door opened  and a very very old woman who supported  herself on crutches came creepin out  Hansel and Gretel was so terribly  frightened that they let for what they  had in their hands  the old woman however nodded her head  and said oh you dear children who has  brought you here do come in and stay  with me no harm shall happen to you she  took them both by the hand and led them  into her little house then good food was  laid before them milk and pancakes with  sugar apples and nuts  afterwards two pretty little beds were  covered with white linen and Hansel and  Gretel laid down in them and thought  they were in heaven the old woman had  only pretended to be so kind she was in  reality a wicked witch who lay in wait  for children and had only built the  little house of bread to entice them  there when a child fell into her power  she killed it cooked and ate it and that  was a feast day to her witches have red  eyes and cannot see far but they have a  keen scent like a beast and are aware of  human beings that draw near when Hansel  and Gretel came into the neighborhood  she laughs maliciously and said  mockingly I have them they will not  escape me again early in the morning  before the children were awake  she was already up and when she saw them  both sleeping looking so pretty with  their plump her cheeks she muttered to  herself that will be a dainty mouthful  then she seized Hansel with her  shriveled hand carried him into a little  stable and shot him in with a grated  door  he might scream as he liked but there  was no use then she went to Gretel shook  her until she awoke and cried get up you  lazy thing fetch some water and cook  something for your brother he is in the  stable outside and is to be made fat  when he is fat I will eat him Gretel  began to weep bitterly but it was all in  vain she was forced to do as the wicked  witch had ordered her and now the best  food was cooked for poor hansel and  gretel got nothing but crab shells every  morning the woman crept up to the stable  and cried Hansel stretch out your finger  that I may feel if you were soon be fat  Hansel however stretched out a little  chicken bone and the old woman who had  demise could not see it and thought it  was Hansel's finger and was astonished  that there was no way of fattening him  when four weeks had gone by and Hansel  still stayed thin she was seized with  impatience and could wait no longer  now Gretel she cried be active and bring  some water  let Hansel be fat or lean tomorrow I  will kill him and cook him and how the  poor little sister did lament when she  had to fetch the water dear God to help  us she cried if the wild beasts in the  forest had devoured us at least we would  have died together early in the morning  Gretel had to go and hang up the core  jhin's of water and light the fire we  will bake first said the old woman I  have already heated the oven and kneaded  the dough she pushed poor Gretel out to  the oven creep in said the witch and see  if it is properly heated so that we may  shut the bread in and once Gretel was  inside she intended to shut the oven and  let her bake in it and then she would  eat her to the Gretel saw what she had  in mind and said I do not know how I am  to do it how do you get in silly goose  said the old woman the door is big  enough just look I can get him myself  and she crept up and thrust her head  into the oven then Gretel gave her a  push that drove her far into it and shut  the I endure and  fasten the bolt she began to howl quite  horribly but Gretel ran away and the  godless witch was miserably burnt to  death  Gretel however ran like lightning to  Hansel opened his stable and cried  Hansel were saved the old witch is dead  then Hansel sprang out like a bird from  a cage when the door was open how they  did rejoice and embrace each other and  dance and kiss each other and as they  had no longer any need to fear her they  went to the witch's house and in every  corner stood chess full of pearls and  jewels these are far better than pebbles  said Hansel and thrust in his pocket  whatever he could get in and Gretel said  I too would take some home with me and  filled her pinafore full but now we will  go away said Hansel that we may get out  of the witch's forest when they had  walked for two hours they came to a  great body of water we cannot get over  said Hansel I seen a foot plank and no  bridge and no boat crosses either  answered Gretel but a white duck is  swimming there if I ask her she will  help us then she cried little duck  little duck  do you see Hansel and Gretel are waiting  for you there's never a plank or bridge  in sight take us across on your back so  white and the duck came to them and  Hansel seated himself on its back and  told his sister to sit by him no replied  Gretel that will be too heavy for the  little duck she shall take us across one  off to the other and the good little  duck did so and when they were safely  across they walked for a short time the  forest seemed to be more familiar to  them and at length they saw from afar  their father's house they began to run  rushed into the parlour and threw  themselves around their father's arms  the man had not known a single happy  hour since he had left his children in  the forest the woman however was dead  Gretel emptied her pinafore until pearls  and precious stones ran about the room  and Hansel threw one handful after  another out of his pocket to enter them  and all anxiety and worry was at an end  and they lived happily together in  perfect  happiness my tail is done see the mouse  run whoever catches it may make himself  a big fur cap out of it and that is a  tale of Hansel and Gretel as told by the  Brothers Grimm  you

